      "problem": "Let $a=65$ and $n=6.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n.$ Then, $r=$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $65$ by $6$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n10<65÷6<11.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=10.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=65-(10)(6)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $65=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65={{10}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 6+{{5}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r=5.$",
